    "done_when": "tests/test_a_fenced_worker_can_commit.py passes at this worktree head against synthetic checkouts under a synthetic code root, never a real one: composing a fence whose worktree equals a protected checkout raises a refusal naming both paths; composing one whose worktree is nested inside a protected checkout also refuses; a linked worktree outside every protected checkout composes as before, and its fenced commit still succeeds; the comment near line 359 states what the code now guarantees; the declared negative control is executed and logged; the gate adds no failures over tests/test_a_fenced_worker_can_commit.py, tests/test_launch_plan_fences_the_home.py and tests/test_each_run_owns_its_harness_home.py against base 7f68e6cc; the change is committed in this worktree",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Make fence_argv in reckon/_backends.py (near line 2157) refuse to compose a fence for a run whose worktree is itself a protected checkout, so the guarantee that a fence never grants a main checkout's own git directory holds, rather than being stated and unmet. The independent review of run r-20260926T074108878140-fence-never-grants-a-main-git-dir (89) measured at 7f68e6cc that a fence composed for a synthetic main checkout carries --ro-bind of the checkout at argv[4], followed by --bind of the same path at argv[7]. The later writable bind of the worktree re-opens the whole checkout, and writes to .git/index and .git/objects succeeded, while the test comment near tests/test_a_fenced_worker_can_commit.py line 359 claims both stay sealed. Dispatch always places a worktree under the separate worktrees root, so this never arises for a dispatched run, but the fence must not silently grant a checkout if it is ever pointed at one. The refusal names the worktree and the protected checkout it coincides with. A linked worktree outside every protected checkout composes exactly as today.",
